@node authentication
@unnumberedsec authentication

To get going@comma{} first set @samp{fj-host}@comma{} and @samp{fj-user}.

To authorize your account@comma{} call @samp{fj-create-token}@comma{} which will prompt you for your password@comma{} create an API access token@comma{} and copy it to your kill ring. Alternatively you can obtain an access token from your profile settings on your Forgejo instance (in user settings@comma{} under ``applications'').

Once you have your token@comma{} call @samp{fj-token} (or any interactive function such as @samp{fj-list-issues}). You will be prompted for your access token@comma{} and when you enter it@comma{} @samp{fj.el} will also offer to save it in your auth-sources file (@samp{~/.authinfo.gpg} or similar; see variable @samp{auth-sources}). Once saved@comma{} you should be good to go.

Alternatively@comma{} you can set @samp{fj-token-use-auth-source} to @samp{nil} and simply set @samp{fj-token} to your token (unencrypted option).

Finally@comma{} if you were previously using the unencrypted @samp{fj-token} method and want to use auth sources@comma{} simply remove the code that sets @samp{fj-token} in your init config@comma{} and follow the steps above.

If you run into any auth issues@comma{} feel free to open an issue or contact me.

@node basic views
@unnumberedsec basic views

Once you are authenticated@comma{} try @samp{fj-list-own-repos} to view your repos@comma{} or @samp{fj-list-issues} to view issues of the current repo@comma{} or with completing-read of a repo if no repo is found.

If you are already viewing a repo and want to force the prompt to choose another@comma{} call @samp{fj-list-issues} with a prefix arg (@samp{C-u}).

You can also call @samp{fj-list-own-issues} to view all issues across your repos@comma{} and @samp{fj-watched-repos} to see your watched repos.

To use a different instance for a given repo@comma{} set @samp{fj-host}@comma{}  @samp{fj-user}@comma{} and @samp{fj-token} in your @samp{.dir-locals.el} file.

@node repositories
@unnumbered repositories

Current repository detection should work partially automatically. If you find you are still being asked for the repo when calling a command from inside a repo with a Forgejo remote@comma{} check to make sure the local root directory matches the name of the Forgejo repo!

